4.4.m:
There I am two-armed Kṛṣṇa along with the gopīs and Rādhā;
here I am accompanied by Kamalā and surrounded by Sunanda and others.
I am Nārāyaṇa and Kṛṣṇa, residing in Śvetadvīpa;
all the gods, Brahmā and the others, are considered to be parts of me alone,
and the gods, asuras, humans, and others are a fraction of a portion of a part of a part.
Go to Goloka, and your objective will be accomplished;
we shall go afterwards for the fulfillment of the desires of all."
Having spoken thus in the midst of the assembly, Hari himself ceased speaking.
Having bowed down, all the deities went to wonderful Goloka,
the extraordinary, supreme abode, which removes old age and death, the transcendent [realm],
to be reached fifty times ten million yojanas above Vaikuṇṭha,
sustained by the wind and created by the sovereign Lord’s own will.
Eager to go to that ineffable realm,
all those who move at the speed of thought reached the bank of the Virajā.
Seeing the riverbank, the gods were filled with the highest wonder:
resembling pure crystal, exceedingly vast, captivating,
endowed with pearls, rubies, and supreme mines of jewels,
adorned with rows of black, white, green, and red gems;
with sprouts of coral springing up here and there, highly charming,
adorned with rows of mines of the finest priceless gems,
unseen even by Brahmā, a marvel, filled with mines of the best treasures;
a mine of rubies and sapphires in some places, O sage,
and in some places endowed with series of mines of emeralds;
in one place a mine of Syamantaka gems, in another a mine of Rucaka gems,
endowed with series of mines of jewels shining with a priceless yellow hue;
in one place a mine of jewels, and in another a mine of Kaustubhas;
in some places a supreme mine of indescribable gems,
and here and there excellent places for delightful pleasure-pastimes.
Seeing this supreme marvel, the lords went to its other side;
they beheld the best of mountains, the charming Śataśṛṅga,

4.6.m:
conversing with kinsmen, and performing my father’s sacrifice.
Then, at an auspicious moment, [there will be] meeting again there with you;
and right there I will see the gopīs.

4.8.m:
The eighth [lunar day] conjoined with the seventh should be avoided with effort;
even if conjoined with the asterism, the eighth conjoined with the seventh must not be observed.
The son of Devakī was born [on an eighth] unpierced [by the seventh] and conjoined with the asterism,
